────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

--output-format stream-json -o out.txt now writes the final answer to the file under the same rules as every other mode. stdout is still a pure NDJSON event stream, and the replaced / wrote response to / was not written notes go to stderr only. fmt, clippy with -D warnings and the full cargo test run all passed before the commit.

Cause: there were two stream-json branches, not one. Both run_single_prompt and the piped (stdin) mode returned before emit_output, the only code that writes the -o file. So both silently skipped the file and exited 0.

Fix: the --output part of emit_output is now its own function, write_output_for_outcome, and every mode calls it. Both stream-json branches now call it after the run ends, with the same failure check as the other modes. That check covers API errors, refusals, unchosen cut-offs and interrupts.

One change outside the task's file list: in src/prompt.rs, the stream-json loop handled Ctrl+C itself but never recorded that it happened. With the fix alone, an interrupted run would have overwritten the file with a half-finished answer and exited 0. A one-line change now records the interrupt, so the old file is kept and the run exits 130, like plain -p.

| case | before | after |
|---|---|---|
| success, no file | no file, nothing on stderr, exit 0 | file holds the answer, "wrote response to", exit 0 |
| success, file exists | file unchanged, nothing on stderr | "replaced out.txt", exit 0 |
| refusal, file exists | kept, no note, exit 1 | kept, "was not written: the run failed", exit 1 |
| HTTP 400, no file | no file, no note | no file, "no file was created" |
| Ctrl+C, file exists | kept, exit 0 | kept, "the run was interrupted", exit 130 |
| piped stdin, success | no file | file holds the answer |

Each case is a test in tests/output_failed_run.rs that runs the built binary against a local stub server, and each checks that every stdout line is valid JSON.

Recall controls:
• The write: disabling it made exactly the 5 new -p stream-json tests fail by name, and putting it back made all 11 pass.
• The piped call site: disabling it alone made only the piped test fail, and putting it back made all 12 pass.

Both before and after results are posted on #1219.

Not tested, only read in the code: under stream-json, SIGTERM, SIGHUP and SIGQUIT end the process immediately, because no handler is installed for them on that path (prompt.rs:1107 is text mode only). The file is left untouched, but nothing on stderr says so. piped-mode.md now says this, along with the fact that the -o rules cover stream-json.

The commit is titled "Fixes #1219", so the push will close the ticket.

